<Vertical id="SimpleTextSelectionDemo" title="TextSelection Block Demo">

  <Markdown>
# TextSelection Block Demo

The TextSelection family lets learners interactively highlight words or phrases
marked in a passage. The terse SimpleTextSelection tag expands to a CapaProblem
wrapping a TextSelectionGrader and a TextSelectionInput; you can also author that
pair by hand for full control.
  </Markdown>

  <!-- 1. Thin tag, immediate mode -->
  <Markdown>
## 1. Immediate Mode (thin tag)

Click words to highlight them; correctness updates live. The articles are marked
`{optional}`, so highlighting them neither helps nor hurts.
  </Markdown>

  <SimpleTextSelection id="demo_immediate" mode="immediate">
Highlight all the nouns:
---
{The} [student] went to {the} [park] with {their} [friends].
  </SimpleTextSelection>

  <!-- 2. Composed by hand, submit mode -->
  <Markdown>
## 2. Graded Mode (composed CapaProblem)

The same pair authored explicitly. Make selections, then Check. Authored
scoring rules pick the feedback message.
  </Markdown>

  <CapaProblem id="demo_graded" grade="submit">
    <TextSelectionGrader>
      <TextSelectionInput>
Identify examples of positive reinforcement:
---
The parents tried [giving extra screen time|screen] when the child behaved well.
They also tried &lt;&lt;yelling&gt;&gt; when frustrated. Later they used [a sticker chart|stickers]
to track progress. Sometimes they would &lt;&lt;take away toys|toys&gt;&gt; as punishment.
---
all: Perfect! You found both positive reinforcement examples! (2/2)
&gt;1: Good start! There's one more example. (1/2)
: Review positive reinforcement - it means adding something pleasant. (0/2)
      </TextSelectionInput>
    </TextSelectionGrader>
  </CapaProblem>

  <!-- 3. Self-Check Mode -->
  <Markdown>
## 3. Self-Check Mode

Make your selections, then reveal the answer to compare.
  </Markdown>

  <SimpleTextSelection id="demo_self_check" mode="selfcheck">
Compare your highlighting with ours:
---
[Reinforcement] increases behavior while [punishment] decreases it.
{Positive and negative} refer to [adding or removing], not good or bad.
  </SimpleTextSelection>

  <!-- 4. Targeted feedback -->
  <Markdown>
## 4. Targeted Feedback

Per-term notes appear as the learner selects specific labeled segments.
  </Markdown>

  <SimpleTextSelection id="demo_advanced" mode="immediate">
Identify the behavioral techniques:
---
They used [positive reinforcement|pos_reinf] by giving rewards. They tried
[negative punishment|neg_punish] by removing toys. The &lt;&lt;yelling&gt;&gt; actually
became {unintentional} [reinforcement|accident].
---
all: Excellent! All techniques identified. (3/3)
&gt;2: Good work! You found most techniques. (2/3)
&gt;1,errors&lt;1: On the right track. (1/3)
: Keep studying the concepts. (0/3)
---
pos_reinf: That's right! Adding something pleasant.
neg_punish: Correct! Removing something the child wants.
accident: Tricky one - the yelling reinforced bad behavior!
yelling: Careful - what effect did the yelling actually have?
  </SimpleTextSelection>

  <Markdown>
## Summary

Interactive text highlighting with three interaction modes, subtractive partial
credit, authored scoring-rule messages, and per-term targeted feedback — for
language arts, psychology, science, and any subject where identifying key
concepts in text matters.
  </Markdown>

</Vertical>
